course: myths
lesson: 10
title: Birds and baby animals
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A fluffy chick hops about on the pavement and Molly flings out her arms: "Don't TOUCH it! Its mum will smell you and never come back!" Everyone freezes, and the chick blinks.
explain: Bird myths are some of the most charming: hummingbirds hitching rides on geese, mother birds shoving their babies out to teach them to fly, wedding rice making birds explode, a duck's quack that mysteriously never echoes.
explain: Most birds have a poor sense of smell, so a touch won't make parents abandon a chick; but Molly's rule is still good, because a fledgling (a young bird that has just left the nest) on the ground usually has its parents close by, watching.
explain: Other tales melt under a test: scientists played a quack in an echo room and heard it echo, and birds eat dry grains every day without exploding.
explain: And watch out for "nobody would believe it" facts, because some are real: an eagle really can spot a rabbit from very far away.
fact: Most birds have a poor sense of smell and won't abandon a chick because a person touched it; still, fledglings on the ground are usually fine and their parents nearby, so it's best to leave them.
fact: Uncooked rice does not make birds explode; birds eat seeds and grains all the time.
fact: Mother birds don't push chicks out to teach flying; fledglings leave when ready, and flying comes from instinct and practice, with parents encouraging.
fact: Hummingbirds migrate on their own, flying amazing distances; they do not ride on geese.
fact: Swans can hit hard and you should keep away from nesting swans, but there is little evidence of them breaking adults' arms.
fact: Some penguin pairs meet again year after year, but many penguins change partners between seasons; it depends on the species.
fact: A duck's quack does echo; it is just quiet and hard to hear, and scientists tested it in an echo chamber at the University of Salford.
fact: "Bird poop is good luck" is a kind saying to cheer people up, not a fact.
fact: Eagles' eyes are several times sharper than ours, so they can spot prey from very far away.
fact: Pigeons, like many animals, can carry some germs, but the risk to people is low with normal hand-washing; pigeons carried messages in wars and some won medals.
fact: Watching real footage, such as a nest camera, is a good way to test a claim about how birds behave.
exercise: Molly says if you touch a chick, its mum will smell you and abandon it. What's true about most birds? | They have a poor sense of smell and won't abandon it for that | They smell people from a mile away | They abandon any chick that has been seen | They only ever come back if you wash the chick with soap and water
exercise: So what should you usually do with a fledgling hopping on the ground? | Leave it, because its parents are usually nearby | Take it home and feed it bread and milk until it can fly | Put it back up a random tree | Carry it to the nearest pond
exercise: Leo's aunt was told wedding rice makes birds explode. What's true? | Birds eat grains all the time; rice won't make them explode | Rice swells up inside them and bursts their tummies wide open | Only cooked rice is dangerous to birds | Pigeons explode but sparrows don't
exercise: Bella's brother says mother birds push chicks out to teach them to fly. What really happens? | Chicks leave when ready, learning by instinct and practice | Mothers throw them out on a set day | Fathers carry them high up into the sky and then let them drop | Chicks are taught to fly inside the egg
exercise: Uncle Dan says hummingbirds ride on geese when they migrate. What's true? | They migrate on their own, flying long distances | They ride on geese every autumn | They never migrate anywhere | They travel hidden inside the feathers of big eagles
exercise: Hattie's grandad says a swan can break your arm with its wing. What is sensible? | Keep away from nesting swans, though the arm story is likely exaggerated | Swans break arms every day, so run indoors | Swans are harmless, you can stroke nesting ones | Only black swans can hit hard
exercise: A cartoon says all penguins mate for life with one partner. What's true? | Some pairs reunite, but many penguins change partners; it depends on the species | Every penguin always keeps one partner | Penguins never have partners at all | Penguins change partners every single day
exercise: A viral page says a duck's quack doesn't echo and nobody knows why. What's true? | It does echo; it's just quiet and hard to hear | It's a real mystery that scientists still can't solve | Ducks quack too fast to echo | Only male ducks' quacks echo
exercise: How did scientists check the duck echo claim? | They played a quack in a room built to study echoes | They asked a thousand ducks to quack together on a lake | They read old poems about ducks | They listened to ducks on the telly
exercise: Tariq's mum says bird poop on you is good luck. What is it really? | A kind saying to cheer people up | A real scientific law of good luck | Proof that the birds really like you | A warning that it is going to rain soon
exercise: Mila's cousin says eagles can see a rabbit from far away. Is it a myth? | No, it's mostly true; their eyes are several times sharper than ours | Yes, eagles see only a few metres | Yes, eagles find rabbits only by smell | No, but only at night
exercise: Ben calls pigeons "flying rats full of diseases". What's the fair truth? | They can carry some germs, but the risk is low if you wash your hands | Touching a pigeon always makes you ill | Pigeons carry no germs at all, ever | Pigeons are a kind of rat with wings
exercise: How did pigeons help people in wars? | They carried messages, and some won medals | They dropped little bombs on the enemy's camps | They spied on enemies by talking like parrots | They pulled tiny carts of food to the soldiers
exercise: What's a good way to see how chicks really learn to leave the nest? | Watch a nest camera in spring | Climb up to a nest and push a chick | Ask a cat | Read a fairy tale about birds
test: "omg a baby bird on the path, I'm gonna pick it up and take it home and raise it!!" Best answer? | Usually leave it; its parents are likely watching nearby | Yes, take it home and feed it milk | Throw it gently up into the air so it learns to fly at once | Put it in the bin so the cat can't get it
test: Leo's aunt bans rice at the wedding to save the birds. What would really happen to birds eating the rice? | Nothing special, they eat grains all the time | They would swell up inside and pop like balloons | They would forget how to fly for a whole week or more | They would all fall fast asleep on the church roof
test: Your friend says, "I touched a chick by accident, now it's doomed!" What do you tell her? | Its parents won't smell you; but leave it now so they can come back | Yes, it's doomed forever | Wash the chick with soap to remove your smell | Paint it so the mum doesn't notice
test: In a cartoon a tiny hummingbird sits on a goose's back, flying south. What's the real story? | Hummingbirds make their long journeys by themselves | That's exactly how they migrate | Geese carry them only over the sea, then they fly alone | Hummingbirds walk south instead
test: On a walk by the river a swan hisses and spreads its wings near its nest. What should you do? | Back away calmly and give it space | Go closer to have a look at the eggs | Throw a few stones at it to scare it off | Stroke its long neck gently to calm it
test: "Mate, ducks' quacks don't echo, scientists are baffled, read it online." Which question cracks it? | Has anyone actually tested it? | How many legs does a duck have? | Why are ducks yellow in cartoons? | Is it on more than one website?
test: A pigeon poos on your head in town. Your mum laughs: "That's good luck!" What's the wise response? | Laugh along; it's a cheering-up saying, not worth arguing | Prove to her with lots of science that luck isn't real at all | Report the pigeon to the police | Take a photo as proof of luck
test: Your cousin bets you a sweet that eagles can spot a rabbit from far away, expecting you to say it's a myth. What's the smart move? | Check first; it's actually true, so he wins the sweet | Say it's a myth and refuse to look it up anywhere, whatever happens | Agree only if he has a photo | Say eagles have no eyes
test: Two penguin pairs at the zoo: one stays together for years, the other swaps partners. What does that show? | Penguins differ; "all penguins mate for life" is too simple | One of the penguins is broken | The zoo mixed up their names | Penguins only pair up in zoos
test: Why do people keep the "don't touch the chick" rule even though the smell reason is wrong? | Leaving it is still best, because its parents are nearby | Because chicks really are poisonous | Because the law says that a person's smell matters to birds | Because birds can read fingerprints
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the response of most birds to the handling of their young by humans? | They do not abandon the young, their sense of smell being poor | They invariably abandon any young that has been handled by a human | They abandon the young only if it is handled twice | They detect the human scent at great distances
exam: It is given that uncooked rice is thrown at a wedding and subsequently eaten by birds. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | No harm of the kind alleged results, birds habitually eating grain | The birds' stomachs rupture owing to the swelling of the rice | The birds are rendered unable to fly | Only rice that has been cooked is consumed by birds
exam: Identify the means by which young birds acquire the capacity of flight. | Instinct and practice, with encouragement from the parents | Being pushed from the nest by the mother on a fixed day of the year | Instruction delivered while still within the egg | Being carried aloft and released by the father
exam: Ascertain the finding of the investigation conducted concerning the echo of a duck's quack. | The quack was found to produce an echo, albeit a faint one | The quack was found to produce no echo whatsoever in any room | The investigation could not be completed | Only the quacks of male ducks were found to echo
